<article class="env-example" id="06QX">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06QX">Example <span data-tag="06QX">4.7.1.10</span> <span class="named">(Truncated Objects of Kan Complexes)</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex, let $x \in X$ be a vertex, and let $n$ be an integer. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The vertex $x$ is $n$-truncated when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $X$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05EC" href="/tag/05EC">4.7.1.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The inclusion $\{ x\}  \hookrightarrow X$ is an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="055Z" href="/tag/055Z">3.5.9.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> This is a restatement of Corollary <a data-tag="056B" href="/tag/056B">3.5.9.12</a>. If $n \geq -1$, then both conditions are equivalent to the following: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Let $X_0 \subseteq X$ be the connected component containing the vertex $x$. Then the Kan complex $X_0$ is $(n-1)$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> See Proposition <a data-tag="056C" href="/tag/056C">3.5.9.13</a>. </p>
</article>
